*** Resetting replication at the very beginning of the test.

*** Generating a configuration file in JSON format for the Binlog
*** Server utility.

*** Determining binlog file directory from the server.

*** Creating a temporary directory <BINSRV_STORAGE_PATH> for storing
*** binlog files downloaded via the Binlog Server utility.

*** Determining the first binary log name.

*** Creating a table and filling it with some data.
CREATE TABLE t1(id INT UNSIGNED NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT, PRIMARY KEY(id)) ENGINE=InnoDB;
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES(DEFAULT);

*** Starting Binlog Server Utility in background in pull mode.

*** Waiting for the replication source listener port ready to accept
*** connections.

*** Waiting for PBS to start receiving the first binary log.

*** Rotating the binary log on the source and killing the source before
*** any transaction is written to the second binary log.
FLUSH BINARY LOGS;
# Kill the server

*** Restarting the source: it starts the third binary log.
# restart

*** Determining the third binary log name.

*** Adding rows and rotating, so that PBS closes the third binary log.
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES(DEFAULT);
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES(DEFAULT);
FLUSH BINARY LOGS;

*** Determining the fourth binary log name.

*** Waiting for PBS to switch to the fourth binary log, checking that it
*** is still running meanwhile.

*** Sending SIGTERM signal to the Binlog Server Utility and waiting
*** for the process to terminate.

*** Comparing server and stored versions of the first binary log.

*** Comparing server and stored versions of the second binary log (the
*** one the source was killed in, holding no transactions).

*** Comparing server and stored versions of the third binary log (the
*** transactions written after the restart).

*** Dropping the table.
DROP TABLE t1;

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility storage directory.

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility log file.

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility configuration file.
